# Beat: guided walk through bob/logfile against alice/app.desc.
#
# Invocation:
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc
#
# The root type is not in alice/app.desc — it is Bob's own log envelope,
# which reproto recovered no schema for.  The session opens untyped.
# This beat names the structure by hand, enables heat cues, and follows
# the cues as far as alice/app.desc can take them.
#
# Re-pin (must print nothing):
#   protolens --descriptor-set alice/app.desc bob/logfile \
#       --script beats/app.desc script \
#     | grep 'error:'

title: The logfile, app.desc

steps:
  - text: |
      This is bob/logfile opened
      against the descriptors in alice/app.desc.

      bob/logfile is a larger protobuf than bob/capture.
      The root type is unknown — no schema in alice/app.desc
      matches the envelope.  What we see is raw field numbers.

      👋 Press `1` to fold the top level nodes.

    node: /
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - not:
        - visible: /1/1

  - text: |
      Now we have a clearer view.

      Note the TRUNCATED_MESSAGE annotation. 

      The logging process was probably killed in flight and the logfile misses 1024 bytes.
      This explains why protoc was unable to parse it.

      👋 Press `2` to see a bit more of the protobuf.

    fold: ["/ 1"]
    node: /
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - visible: /1/1

  - text: |
      The top-level fields each contain similar submessages.
      They look like log entries indeed — one per RPC call.

      Let's name the entries using the proposed prefilled command below.

      It declares a new synthetic type "Entry", marks field 1 as
      repeated and gives it the name "entries".
      
      👋 Press Enter to commit the Entry type override.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "1 [{]  #@ message"
    command: override /:1 --as-new Entry --cardinality repeated --field-name entries
    advance_when:
      - type: /1 Entry

  - text: |
      👍 Good, the Entry type override is in place.
       
      To go futher, let's enable protolens heat cues.
      Protolens will tell us where in the protobuf it recognizes known types.
      Heat cues show as green/amber/blue annotations.

      👋 Press `i` to enable heat cues.

    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /
    search: "entries [{]  #@ repeated Entry"
    advance_when:
      - heat_cues: findings

  - text: |
      We see that field 5 of the first entry has a bright heat cue.
      Hover over it. Notice it proposes two matching types.
      
      Let's pick SearchTextResponse, it feels right and corresponds to what was in bob/capture.
      
      👋 Double-click on the heat-cue, then double-click on SearchTextResponse.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/4
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/4 google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextResponse

  - text: |
      👍 Good, the SearchTextResponse override is in place.

      Now hover over the amber heat-cue of field 4.
      It proposes a single matching type.
      
      Let's accept SearchTextRequest.
      
      👋 Double-click on the heat-cue, then press Enter to commit the override.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/3
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/3 google.maps.places.v1.SearchTextRequest

  - text: |
      👍 OK, SearchTextRequest is also in place.

      What about field 2?
      It looks like the name of the logged RPC.
      Let's keep the string type and simply rename it "rpc".

      👋 Press Enter to commit the prefilled command.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2"]
    node: /1/2
    select_line: true
    command: override Entry:2 --field-name rpc
    advance_when:
      - field_name: /1/2 rpc

  - text: |
      Now what about field 1?
      Let's hover over the heat cue...
      Hmm... Timestamp is probably right, for a log entry.
      
      👋 Press `t` to open the override selection pane, then
         choose Timestamp and press Enter to commit the override.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /1/1
    select_line: true
    advance_when:
      - type: /1/1 google.protobuf.Timestamp

  - text: |
      👌 Perfect

      Now let's have a look at the 3rd node, with fields number 6 and 7.
      Fields 6 shows a deeply negative score 😨.
      And hovering above the heat cues gives nothing convincing. 🤔🤔🤔

      👋 Press space to step.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      🤔 Nothing fits...

      Maybe the app.desc descriptors do not include what's needed...
      Now SearchTextRequest/Response are from the googleapis descriptors.
      
      Maybe we can try by re-opening bob/logfile,
      this time against the full googleapis.desc?

      👋 Press space to step.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    select_line: true

  - text: |
      ✋ Before doing that, let's save our overrides:
         we don't want to have to redo this work.

      👋 Press Enter to save the overrides to disk.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    node: /3/3
    command: save-overrides alice/overrides
    advance_when:
      - file_exists: alice/overrides

  - text: |
      Now press Enter to quit.

    heat_cues: findings
    fold: ["/ 2", "/1/1 Z"]
    command: quit
